SUMMARY:Applied Mathematics Seminar: Keren Li — Representative Learning: 
 From Point Representatives to Distributed AI
LOCATION:GP 346\, The University of Alabama\, Tuscaloosa\, Alabama
DESCRIPTION:Keren Li\, Assistant Professor\, Department of Mathematics\, Un
 iversity of Alabama at Birmingham\nRepresentative Learning: From Point Rep
 resentatives to Distributed AI\nDistributed machine learning traditionally
  relies on exchanging gradients\, model parameters\, or predictions across
  computing nodes. Such communication can become inefficient and unstable w
 hen data are heterogeneous or communication is asynchronous. In this talk\
 , I present Representative Learning (RepL)\, a framework that replaces mod
 el-based communication with the exchange of representative data objects th
 at summarize local statistical information while preserving privacy and in
 terpretability.\n\nI will describe the evolution of RepL from simple point
  representatives to score-preserving and distribution-valued representatio
 ns\, highlighting recent developments in stability\, identifiability\, and
  heterogeneity-aware learning. I will also discuss extensions to collabora
 tive learning\, clouded representatives based on lifted empirical risk\, a
 nd distributed AI systems that communicate information at the data level r
 ather than the model level. The talk concludes with current challenges and
  future directions toward a unified framework for distributed AI under het
 erogeneity and communication constraints.\nhttps://jiazhaomath.github.io/s
 eminar/2026-2027.html#2026-10-02
URL:https://jiazhaomath.github.io/seminar/2026-2027.html#2026-10-02
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
